---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Docs
  title="stack add"
  description="Set up one provider, attempt credential and MCP/config writes, and optionally install its SDK. Cleanup on failure is best-effort."
  eyebrow="CLI"
>
  <h2>Basic usage</h2>

  <Code
    code={`stack add supabase
stack add clerk
stack add resend`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    For the full flag reference, see the <a href="/docs/cli/">CLI reference</a>.
    This page covers the three behaviours that aren't obvious from the synopsis:
    optional SDK installation, best-effort rollback, and env export.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sdk-install">SDK auto-install</h2>
  <p>
    After provisioning and persisting a service's secrets, Stack checks whether
    the provider ships an SDK package (e.g. <code>@supabase/supabase-js</code>,
    <code>@clerk/nextjs</code>). If it does, Stack detects your project's package
    manager and prompts once:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`◇  Install @supabase/supabase-js? (detected: bun)
│  Yes / No (illustrative prompt)`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Detection order: <code>bun.lock</code> → <code>pnpm-lock.yaml</code> →
    <code>yarn.lock</code> → <code>package-lock.json</code> → <code>package.json</code>.
    Falls back to <code>npm</code> if none are found.
  </p>

  <p>Override the prompt with the <code>--install</code> flag:</p>

  <Code
    code={`# Always install without asking
stack add supabase --install=always

# Never install (useful in CI or monorepos where you manage deps separately)
stack add supabase --install=never

# Default: prompt once (same as omitting the flag)
stack add supabase --install=ask`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <h2 id="transactional-rollback">Best-effort cleanup</h2>
  <p>
    <code>stack add</code> runs a multi-step pipeline: provider auth → resource creation or attachment → materialization →
    credential writes → MCP merge → split-config writes. New managed login
    inputs are staged; resource-critical credentials can be committed before
    materialization completes. On failure, Stack attempts cleanup of its owned
    writes and newly created resources. Existing resources and stored keys are
    separate, and partial state can remain.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Illustrative failure; inspect the actual cleanup result
┌  stack add neon
│
◆  Provisioning neon...
│  ✓ Created project "my-app" (ep-quiet-frost-a1b2c3d4)
│  ✗ Failed to write DATABASE_URL to Phantom: vault locked
│
◆  Rolling back...
│  ✓ Deleted neon project ep-quiet-frost-a1b2c3d4
│
└  ✗ neon was not added. Phantom vault must be unlocked.`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Supabase, Neon, Turso and Vercel have resource cleanup implementations,
    but cleanup can fail or refuse. Vault removal requires the installed
    Phantom interface and confirmation; headless cleanup may keep keys.
    Inspect the original failure, retained resource references and cleanup
    result before deciding on manual recovery.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    Use <code>--dryRun</code> to inspect the add plan before provider setup.
    The add parser has no rollback-disable flag; a failed real setup is not
    proof that all earlier effects were undone.
  </div>

  <p>
    Swap has its own setup and local cleanup sequence; it is not atomic. See <code>stack swap</code>. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/swap/">Provider swap</a> for details.
  </p>

  <h2 id="env-export">Generating <code>.env.example</code></h2>
  <p>
    After adding services, generate an empty-value <code>.env.example</code> that
    lists declared secret keys with empty values for teammates and CI:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`stack env export --example`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <Code
    code={`# .env.example — illustrative empty-key skeleton; review before sharing
# Values are placeholders only. Run \`stack add <service>\` to provision.

SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    The file is written to <code>.env.example</code> in the current working
    directory. Use <code>--stdout</code> to print the skeleton, or
    <code>--force</code> to overwrite an existing example. Dedicated values
    are not read from Phantom; the command emits catalog or declared key names
    with empty values. Review user-supplied names before sharing.
  </p>
</Docs>
